ral government  no i haven't that surprised you  yeah it does um as a matter of fact i i  did call the local public health  authorities when when this  all first began and checked in with them  and said hey  um here's my job here's what i'm being  asked to do  i understand what your orders are and am  i in violation of your orders  by following the directives of my  company  and i was told absolutely i was however  getting anybody to investigate that  was was something that seemed next to  impossible  which is at that point when i decided to  contact an attorney  i want to thank you both for being here  very much we continue to follow the  story and  keep us posted on any reaction or  developments in the case  thank you both very much thank you  in a statement to us from venus the  company said its representatives are in  fact critical  and that the company follows all health  guidelines at the state local  and federal level coming up next winners  and losers this week  in the media see what the editors of  mediaide had to say about the  prestigious  atlantic magazine you're watching brian  ross investigates on the long crime  trial network  time now for this week's winners and  losers in the media  as chosen by the editors at mediaite and  we're joined by the editor-in-chief of  meteorite aidan mclaughlin  media of course like the law and crime  trial network is part of the dan abrams  media empire and aiden let's start with  your first winner a surprise i thought  the new york post that's right so the  new york post regained access to their  twitter account  this week after they were locked out by  the platform  for posting their hunter biden story now  i want to  bring this to you as a winner with an  enormous caveat this is by no means an  endorsement of  the new york post hunter biden reporting  uh which was effectively  oppo research uh that was pushed by uh  allies of president donald trump  and laundered through the new york post  given a sort of tabloid sensibility  uh the post came to some sort of you  know conclusions about that story and  about uh  joe biden's relation to hunter biden's  business dealings  that no other serious news outlet came  to those same conclusions that was  rejected by the wall street journal and  many other outlets passed on this story  but twitter  locked new york post out of its account  because it was publishing tweets that  included the story  in it and i think that was a step too  far and twitter actually got a lot of  criticism for this um you know the new  york post published a tabloid story as  the new york post  does um this story you know the hunter  biden thing is not that much different  than a lot of the more sensational  stories we had about  the trump campaign in russia a couple  years ago so  for twitter to sort of arbitrarily  mandate which stories get  uh sort of censored by the platform was  really a bad idea and twitter realized  it  and gave the new york post access back  to its account  of course but by banning it that gave it  new oxygen and it spread elsewhere  that became a more legitimate story than  the actual story they were  talking about totally the i mean the new  york post story i don't think would have  made  half the splash it did had twitter and  facebook not acted to restrict its reach  um so i think it sort of outlines the  absurdity of these social media  platforms trying to select  which news stories they approve of and  which ones they don't uh it also made  that new york post story on hunter biden  one of the biggest new york post stories  of the year  uh and they they ran you know several  covers on the basis of it  right let's move now to a loser this is  a switch this year  the loser of most prestigious magazine  atlantic magazine chosen as one of your  losers why is that right i think it was  it was an interesting selection this  week that we had a tabloid like the new  york post as a winner and  a venerable magazine like the atlantic  is the loser but the atlantic  was forced to print a massive 800 word  correction  to a story that came out this week uh or  last week and it was also in the  magazine  and they eventually retracted the story  altogether this week  it was a long-form dive the piece into  parents in a tony connecticut suburb  were pushing their children into niche  sports to give them a leg up when  applying to colleges that's what the  story was about  but the piece was written by ruth  charlotte who back in the 1990s was  a pretty popular magazine writer she  wrote her gq  she was a writer at the new republic  before she flamed out in this pretty  shocking plagiarism scandal uh now after  she submitted this piece to the atlantic  they apparently  said you know it's been decades she's  been published elsewhere we're  comfortable publishing this piece by her  they ran their fact checking process on  it which was very thorough  but a washington post media critic eric  wempel actually looked into the story  and found a series of inaccuracies on it  which were quite glaring  and the atlantic then did their second  fact check and they deemed that the  writer had deceived them  they published this massive and scathing  800 word correction to the piece  eventually retracted it it's a pretty it  you know it's a bruise for a magazine  that  has actually a very good reputation for  printing factual stories  um but i i have to say it sounds like  they should have known better  and they seem to have acknowledged bad  judgment  for sure yeah they said that that ruth  charlotte deceived them but that they  should have known that uh you know to  backtrack this more clearly and you know  one of the mistakes was a pretty glaring  one  uh one of ruth charlotte's top sources  said that she had a daughter and told  some of these elaborate stories about  her daughter's sporting activities and  then revealed that she  actually didn't have a daughter and the  atlantic said that even the the writer  may have actually pushed  her source to deceive her  amazing amazing ladies mclaughlin thank  you so much for joining us we'll be  looking forward to seeing you next week  to talk about election coverage  that's it for tonight thank you so much  for joining us and thanks for the great  team at law and crime  for getting us on and off the air  [Music]  so  you
 

